;; Genesis 1. Format: gsb/translation/README.md. Check: make gsb/lint

(note "**Opening.** Swete prints the first two words in capitals without accents (ΕΝ ΑΡΧΗ); they are ἐν ἀρχῇ, rendered \"In [the] beginning\" with the same concordance glosses as John 1:1, which opens with the same two words. The table has no lemma for ΕΝ, so it is supplied as ἐν.")

(note "**The fiat formula.** God's commands use the Greek third-person imperative: γενηθήτω φῶς, literally \"let come to be light\". The literal layer puts the subject between \"Let\" and the verb, as English requires (\"Let light come to be\", \"Let the earth sprout\", 1:11): both pieces of English belong to the one Greek verb, and hovering either shows the other. The answering ἐγένετο is \"came to be\", the same verb and gloss as John 1:3 (\"all [things] through him came to be\"). The readable layer uses the familiar \"Let there be ... and there was\".")

(note "**Refrains.** καὶ ἐγένετο οὕτως is \"and it came to be so\" (readable \"And it was so\"); καὶ ἴδεν ὁ θεὸς ὅτι καλόν is \"and God saw that [it was] good\"; καὶ ἐγένετο ἑσπέρα καὶ ἐγένετο πρωί, ἡμέρα μία is \"and evening came to be, and morning came to be: day one\". The days are anarthrous in Greek (\"day one\", \"[a] second day\" ... \"[a] sixth day\"); the article in the readable layer's \"a second day\" is English, not Greek. Swete spells εἶδεν as ἴδεν throughout; the table leaves it unlemmatized, and it is supplied as ὁράω, as the New Testament table lemmatizes εἶδον.")

(note "**Differs from the Hebrew.** The Septuagint's Genesis 1 is more regular than the Hebrew text behind most English Bibles (this summary is from the translator's own knowledge of the Hebrew, for checking). It has \"and God saw that [it was] good\" on the second day (1:8), which the Hebrew lacks; it reports the gathering of the waters a second time (1:9b); it places \"and it came to be so\" at the end of 1:6 (Hebrew: end of 1:7) and adds it at 1:20; it adds \"for illumination of the earth, and to rule the day and the night\" to 1:14 and \"and all the livestock and all the earth\" to 1:28. Each place has its own note.")

(note "**ἄνθρωπος and Adam.** Genesis 1 says ἄνθρωπος, \"[a] human\" (1:26), \"the human\" (1:27), the word for a human being of either sex. The literal layer keeps \"human\"; the readable layer says \"man\", the English convention for the first human and for humankind. The name Ἀδάμ does not appear until 2:16; see the Genesis book notes.")

